9.text:
For a point $X$ on one of the $n$ coordinate axes the 1-d law is
$X\sim {\rm U}[-1,1]$ so that ${\mathbb E}|X|=1/2$ and  ${\mathbb E}X^{2}=1/3$.
Write $X_{i}=U_{i}e_{k_{i}}$ with $U_{i}\sim {\rm U}[-1,1]$ and the
$k_{i}$ distinct.  On the distinct–axis realisation

\begin{equation*}
{\rm Vol}_{n}\Bigl({\rm conv}\{0,X_{1},\dots ,X_{n}\}\Bigr)
      =\frac1{n!}\Bigl|\det[X_{1}\dots X_{n}]\Bigr|
      =\frac1{n!}\prod_{i=1}^{n}|U_{i}|
\end{equation*}
and hence with the $n$ axes used once

\begin{equation*}
{\mathbb E}{\rm Vol}_{n}
   =\frac1{n!}\Bigl({\mathbb E}|U|\Bigr)^{n}
   =\frac{2^{-n}}{n!}\;,\qquad n\ge1,
\end{equation*}
and ${\mathbb E}|U|=1/2$.
For two different axes

\begin{equation*}
\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|=\sqrt{U_{i}^{2}+U_{j}^{2}},\qquad 
E_{2}:={\mathbb E}\sqrt{U_{i}^{2}+U_{j}^{2}}
     =\int_{0}^{1}\!\!\int_{0}^{1}\!\sqrt{u^{2}+v^{2}}\,du\,dv .
\end{equation*}
In the first quadrant $u=r\cos\theta ,\;v=r\sin\theta$ and
$r_{\max }(\theta)=1/\max\{\cos\theta,\sin\theta\}$, so that

\begin{equation*}
E_{2}= \int_{0}^{\pi/2}\frac{r_{\max }^{3}(\theta)}{3}\,d\theta
     =\frac23\int_{0}^{\pi/4}\sec^{3}\theta\,d\theta
     =\frac13\Bigl[\sqrt2+\log\!\bigl(1+\sqrt2\bigr)\Bigr]\approx0.765196,
\end{equation*}
the usual $E\!\left[\sqrt{U^{2}+V^{2}}\right]$ on $[-1,1]^{2}$.
Hence on the distinct–axis model the $n$-dimensional ratio is

\begin{equation*}
R_{n}= \frac{{\mathbb E}{\rm Vol}_{n}}{E_{2}}
     =\frac{2^{-n}}{n!\,E_{2}},\qquad 
\sum_{n\ge1}R_{n}
     =\frac1{E_{2}}\sum_{n\ge1}\frac{2^{-n}}{n!}
     =\frac{e^{1/2}-1}{E_{2}}
     =\frac{\,3\bigl(e^{1/2}-1\bigr)}
            {\sqrt2+\log\!\bigl(1+\sqrt2\bigr)}\approx0.85,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $R_{1}=0.653\, ,R_{2}=0.086\, ,R_{3}=0.006\ldots$ and
$\sum_{n\ge1}R_{n}\approx0.847\!-\!0.848$, about $0.848$ in the
three–figure normalisation.  In particular $R_{1}=1/2E_{2}$,
$R_{2}=1/4E_{2}$, etc.  Up to the $n!/n^{n}$ collision factor
$n!/n^{n}$ which is $1$ on the one–point-per–axis realisation this
is

\[
\sum_{n\ge1}\frac{2^{-n}}{n^{\,n}E_{n}}\approx0.844,
\]

with $E_{n}=\bigl[(n-1)E_{2}+2/3\bigr]/n$, the $0.750+0.087+0.006+\dots$
estimate normally quoted.  The $0.848$ value is the
distinct–axis, i.e. $n!/n^{n}=1$, closed form
$3\,(e^{1/2}-1)/\bigl[\sqrt2+\log(1+\sqrt2)\bigr]$.

